<Window x:Class="EQBuddy.ShellWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="EQBuddy"
        Background="{DynamicResource BgBrush}" WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen">
    <!-- THE EVOLVED SHELL HOST (E-3 Phase 2 PR 1). Bevel's shell nav pre-design,
         Helm-signed 2026-09-04 ~9:25 PM CT.

         **NATIVE CHROME, AND IT IS THE FIRST DECISION RATHER THAN A DETAIL.** Every
         theme window in this repo — ProgressWindow, QuestsWindow, GearLootWindow,
         CreatureWindow, WorldWindow, OptionsWindow — is
         `WindowStyle="None" AllowsTransparency="True" Topmost="True" ShowInTaskbar="False"`
         with a hand-drawn OnDrag standing in for the title bar. They are shaped that way
         because they were born as POP-OUTS OF AN OVERLAY that sits over the game. The
         shell is not: it is a normal Windows window and is what Windows tabs to, which is
         the pre-design's gate 7. Copying the theme-window header here would have shipped
         that gate broken on day one, and NOTHING ABOUT IT WOULD SHOW IN A DIFF — it would
         be a Window tag with the same four attributes every other window in the folder
         already has, which is exactly how a copy-paste default becomes invisible.

         HistoryWindow.xaml is the precedent and it has been sitting there the whole time:
         a plain resizable Window with native chrome, a taskbar entry and working Alt+Tab,
         because nobody ever needed it to float over the game. So this window carries no
         drag handler, no hand-rolled close button and no custom corner radius — those are
         DELETED rather than ported, the same call the World fold made for four cog menu
         entries. The title-bar icon is the app icon, which native chrome draws for free.

         Sizes: MinWidth/MinHeight AND the opening Width/Height all come from
         ShellLayoutPolicy in code, not from literals here. The floor because it is DERIVED
         (a room's minimum plus the collapsed rail); the opening size because it is an INPUT
         to the secondary-monitor placement below — "is there a band wide enough to hold
         this window" — and a number typed here and again in the test that asks that question
         is a number that will disagree with itself, silently, with both sides internally
         consistent.

         WindowStartupLocation is the ONE-MONITOR ANSWER, not the rule. CenterScreen means
         the PRIMARY screen, which is where EverQuest is — so the constructor overrides it
         with Manual and a secondary-monitor origin whenever the desk has a display beside
         the primary one. Left here rather than replaced because null from
         ScreenGuard.SecondaryOrigin means "you decide", and this is the decision: a
         one-monitor desk (and a 1024×768 CI runner) keeps exactly the behaviour it had.

         Nothing below carries a literal font size, radius or spacing value — every number
         resolves out of the token dictionary DesignSystem.Tokens() composes from
         EQBuddy.UI.Shared.DesignTokens. DesignRatchetTests fails the build if a literal
         reappears; this surface joins that list on the day it lands rather than later. -->
    <Grid>
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- TITLE ROW. Search lives HERE and not on the rail, and the reason is a
             product one: a rail row says "this is one of the rooms you navigate to", and
             Search is how you SKIP the rooms rather than a domain of its own. Putting it
             in the rail — even visually separated — still teaches "there are eight rows".
             The window's own name is drawn beside it because native chrome puts the title
             in the title bar, where it is small and far from the content. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <StackPanel x:Name="TitleRow" Grid.Column="0" Orientation="Horizontal"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <Grid x:Name="SearchBox" Grid.Column="2" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
        </Grid>

        <Grid Grid.Row="1">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition x:Name="RailColumn" Width="Auto"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <!-- THE RAIL — vertical, not a horizontal tab strip, and the reason is
                 capacity rather than taste. This codebase has already paid twice to learn
                 that a horizontal strip clips its overflow silently with no ellipsis and
                 no error (trap 14, then trap 25 with chips, on FOUR peers). The shell has
                 seven rooms plus a Search affordance, and the rooms next to them want
                 room to breathe. A rail grows DOWN.

                 It is a Grid of two rows rather than one stack so Settings can sit under
                 a gap at the bottom: it configures the tool, where the others describe
                 the character. That split holds at EVERY width — collapsing the rail to
                 icons must never also reorder or drop a room, which would turn a resize
                 into a silent capability loss. -->
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Background="{DynamicResource PanelBrush}"
                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource HairlineBrush}" BorderThickness="0,0,1,0">
                <Grid x:Name="Rail">
                    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                        <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
                        <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                    </Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <StackPanel x:Name="RailRows" Grid.Row="0" Margin="{DynamicResource StackS}"/>
                    <StackPanel x:Name="RailBelowGap" Grid.Row="1" Margin="{DynamicResource StackS}"/>
                </Grid>
            </Border>

            <!-- THE ROOM. The host owns the bounds and the room owns everything inside
                 them: no Visibility and no Margin here, because a control that hides
                 itself inside a host that also hides itself has two switches for one
                 state and only one of them is ever wired (trap 15). Scrolling belongs to
                 the room, which gets a bounded * cell to overflow inside (trap 36 is
                 about a child scroller measured with INFINITE height; this one is not). -->
            <Grid Grid.Column="1">
                <ContentControl x:Name="RoomHost"/>

                <!-- The Ctrl+K palette, drawn OVER the room rather than replacing it —
                     Search is a shortcut past the nav, so landing on it must not cost you
                     the room you were reading. Collapsed until asked for. -->
                <Border x:Name="PaletteLayer" Visibility="Collapsed"
                        Background="{DynamicResource BgBrush}"
                        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource HairlineBrush}" BorderThickness="1"
                        CornerRadius="{DynamicResource CornerPanel}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
                    <StackPanel x:Name="PaletteBody" Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}"/>
                </Border>
            </Grid>
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</Window>
